After any condition in the if ڕ else if ڕ else chain evaluates to true, all subse-
quent conditions are no longer evaluated (i.e., the rest of the chain is shorted). In the
preceding code, Condition 1 is false, so Condition 2 is checked. Because Condition 2 is
true, the computer will completely skip Condition 3.
Nesting if Statements
You can nest if statements inside of each other for more complex behavior:
bool night = true;
bool fullMoon = false;
if (!night) {
print( "It's daytime. Why don't you go outside and enjoy yourself?" );
} else {
if (fullMoon) {
print( "Watch for werewolves!!!" );
} else {
print( "It's night, but the moon is not full." );
}
}
// The output of this code will be:
// It's night, but the moon is not full.
sZitch Statements
A sZitch statement can take the place of several if ڕ else statements, but it has
some strict limitations:
1. Switch statements can only compare for equality.
2. Switch statements can only compare a single variable (e.g., nuP in the following
code).
3. Switch statements can only compare that variable against literals (not other
variables).
